WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.190
If you don't like your accent and
have spent time trying to modify it

00:00:04.190 --> 00:00:08.500
and change it to resemble an 
anglophone accent,

00:00:08.500 --> 00:00:10.300
then this video is for you.

00:00:10.300 --> 00:00:14.300
In today's video, we're diving 
straight in with some stats

00:00:14.300 --> 00:00:17.650
as to why you should 
embrace your accent.

00:00:17.660 --> 00:00:21.360
If you haven't seen Episode One,
then go ahead and watch it with

00:00:21.360 --> 00:00:24.870
the link up here and then make
sure you come right back.

00:00:24.880 --> 00:00:29.515
Why should you embrace your accent
when you speak in English?

00:00:29.525 --> 00:00:33.545
Reason Number 3: Because most
English speakers in the world

00:00:33.555 --> 00:00:36.005
don't have an Anglophone accent.

00:00:36.015 --> 00:00:38.705
Let me give you a quick maths 
and history lesson.

00:00:38.705 --> 00:00:43.605
According to the 2021 edition of 
Ethnologue: Languages of the World,

00:00:43.605 --> 00:00:48.605
there are 978.2 million people 
across the globe

00:00:48.605 --> 00:00:51.305
who speak English 
as a second language.

00:00:51.313 --> 00:00:58.108
That's compared to 369.9 million for 
whom English is their mother tongue.

00:00:58.120 --> 00:01:03.100
In other words, more English speakers
don't have a native accent

00:01:03.110 --> 00:01:06.000
than those that do. Thank you 
and good night.

00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:10.000
I'm going to keep those numbers 
up on screen so they really sink in.

00:01:10.100 --> 00:01:17.120
Number of people who speak English 
as a second language: 978.2 million.

00:01:17.120 --> 00:01:23.060
Number of people who speak English as 
their mother tongue: 369.9 million.

00:01:23.070 --> 00:01:29.930
That's 264% more people who speak
English as a second language.

00:01:29.940 --> 00:01:33.740
Put another way, for every one person
who speaks English as their

00:01:33.740 --> 00:01:39.180
mother tongue, 2.6 people speak
English as their second language.

00:01:39.190 --> 00:01:43.120
You get the point. Unless you
live in an anglophone country,

00:01:43.270 --> 00:01:47.160
it's likely that most of the people
you interact with will have

00:01:47.170 --> 00:01:51.000
a non-anglophone accent. So 
embrace your accent.

00:01:51.000 --> 00:01:56.100
Reason Number 4: Embracing your 
accent will show your employer

00:01:56.100 --> 00:01:59.500
that you are a hard worker. We 
covered this in episode one,

00:01:59.500 --> 00:02:01.900
so I'm not going to repeat 
the whole point again,

00:02:01.900 --> 00:02:05.000
but learning a second 
language is hard work.

00:02:05.000 --> 00:02:07.560
Don't hide the fact that 
you've put that work in.

00:02:07.570 --> 00:02:12.990
It's important to note that most
jobs will not require you to

00:02:12.990 --> 00:02:16.000
have an anglophone accent when
you're speaking.

00:02:16.000 --> 00:02:20.070
Usually, employers prioritise fluency 
and confidence when speaking

00:02:20.070 --> 00:02:23.200
in English over a particular accent.

00:02:23.200 --> 00:02:24.580
I would strongly suggest that

00:02:24.580 --> 00:02:28.190
you spend your time developing your
confidence and your fluency

00:02:28.190 --> 00:02:32.420
when speaking in English rather
than trying to modify and change

00:02:32.420 --> 00:02:36.090
your accent to resemble an 
anglophone accent. It takes a lot

00:02:36.090 --> 00:02:40.440
of time to change the way you speak.
Ask any speech therapist.

00:02:40.450 --> 00:02:44.500
Spend that time on strengthening
other skills. This is a really

00:02:44.500 --> 00:02:47.010
important subject because it's
something that a lot of people

00:02:47.010 --> 00:02:51.180
worry about. So we've written
a whole blog post on the subject

00:02:51.180 --> 00:02:55.140
of embracing your accent. So do
me a favour. Go and read it,

00:02:55.150 --> 00:02:59.220
then embrace your accent. Stop worrying
about it and start spending

00:02:59.220 --> 00:03:03.370
your time on the other core
English language skills.

00:03:10.080 --> 00:03:14.130
That's everything for our embracing
your accent series. If you

00:03:14.130 --> 00:03:17.580
want to see more videos like this
then make sure you subscribe.

00:03:17.590 --> 00:03:20.990
We have videos coming out to help
you with Business English,

00:03:21.000 --> 00:03:24.830
and in the meantime, embrace
your accent, embrace its tones,

00:03:24.840 --> 00:03:28.860
embrace its textures, and I
will see you next time. Bye

